# Lesson 45: Integration Activities — Designing a Digital Services and Learning Programme

> ACTIVITY:
  **Context.** The Parent–Teacher Association of your school wants to (a) move fee payment, results consultation and enrolment online, (b) partner with the district hospital for a student e-health card and vaccination reminders, (c) help parents use e-government services, and (d) launch a blended e-learning programme that works with unreliable internet. They need a feasibility study and a demonstrator.
  **Task (groups of 4, 2 periods).**
  1. **E-services design** (Lesson 42): describe how the school's e-services would operate – enrolment form → validation → fee payment via MoMo/Orange Money → receipt → results portal; draw the process flowchart with authentication and payment gateway; specify channels (web, USSD/SMS, agent desk) for parents without smartphones; identify the e-health integration (appointment/reminder flow, consent and data protection) and two e-government services parents most need (tax form, civil status/ID, exam registration) with step-by-step guides.
  2. **Benefits, challenges and usability** (Lesson 43): a stakeholder benefit/challenge matrix (parents, school, hospital, ministry) with mitigations; a usability/accessibility audit of one existing platform the school already depends on (results portal or a bank/MoMo app) using the checklist, with a scorecard and improvements; a bilingual, mobile-first wireframe (paper or Canva/Figma) of the enrolment-and-payment page applying validation messages, progress bar and accessibility rules.
  3. **E-learning delivery plan** (Lesson 44): choose and justify the delivery mix – cloud LMS (Google Classroom/Moodle), offline content server (Raspberry Pi with Kiwix/Kolibri) and e-books (PDF/EPUB OER), plus WhatsApp/printed fallbacks; budget under 500,000 XAF; teacher-training outline; evaluation criteria for the resources; comparison with the current traditional approach and expected benefits/limitations.
  4. **Demonstrator**: (a) a working demo enrolment form with validation (Google Forms/Sheets or a spreadsheet with Data Validation) and a simulated payment confirmation e-mail; (b) a demo course in Classroom/Moodle with one resource, one quiz and one assignment; (c) if possible, a Kiwix/Kolibri page served on the lab network or an EPUB opened on a phone; (d) a short screen recording or live walk-through.
  5. **Deliver** a 6–8 page feasibility report (PDF, produced collaboratively with version history) and an 8-minute presentation with the demonstrator; e-mail the report to the teacher with proper etiquette.
  **Criteria.** Correct and complete e-service process design with inclusive channels and data-protection awareness; rigorous stakeholder analysis and evidence-based platform audit; realistic, costed e-learning delivery plan that fits local constraints; working demonstrator; quality of report, wireframe and presentation; collaboration and communication evidence.

## Rubric

| Criterion | 4 – Excellent | 3 – Good | 2 – Developing | 1 – Beginning |
| E-services process design | Complete flows (enrolment, payment, results, e-health, e-gov guides), inclusive channels, security/consent addressed | Complete with minor gaps | Partial | Incoherent |
| Stakeholder analysis & audit | Rich matrix with mitigations; audit with scores, evidence and prioritised fixes; strong wireframe | Good analysis, adequate audit | Superficial | Missing |
| E-learning delivery plan | Justified mix, costed, training and evaluation criteria, honest limitations | Justified, less detailed | Generic | Unrealistic |
| Demonstrator | Form with validation, confirmation e-mail, demo course, offline/e-book element working | Most elements work | One element | None |
| Report, presentation & collaboration | Professional, bilingual-aware, within time, clear collaboration evidence, proper e-mail | Clear | Basic | Weak |

