= First Term Consolidation and Revision

## Term overview

| Week | Lessons | Module / theme | Key competences |
| 1 | 1–4 | AI: concept, ethics, techniques, machine learning | Define/history/types of AI; responsible use; intelligent systems; build and test an ML model |
| 2 | 5–8 | Developing AI systems; Integration; network fundamentals | AI development stages/tools; network vs stand-alone; complexity, benefits, limits, interoperability |
| 3 | 9–12 | Integration; network types; choosing a network; Integration | LAN/MAN/WAN/CAN/PAN comparison; network selection for an organisation |
| 4 | 13–16 | Architecture; topologies; comparing topologies; Integration | P2P vs client/server; mesh/star/ring/bus/tree; topology choice |
| 5 | 17–18 (+ Eval 1) | Network equipment; Evaluation/Remediation 1; setting up a P2P network | Modem/hub/switch/router/NOS; practical set-up and test |
| 6 | 19–22 | Selecting equipment; Integration; intranet/extranet; VPNs & privacy | Equipment selection; intranet/extranet design; VPN, firewall, digital signature set-up |
| 7 | 23–26 | Security (CIA); data protection & access control; validation vs verification; Integration | Threats and controls; encryption/permissions; data checks |
| 8 | 27–30 | Transmission modes; broadband/narrowband; transmission types; media | CSMA/CD–CA, duplex modes; bandwidth classes; serial/parallel, sync/async, analog/digital; guided/unguided media |
| 9 | 31–34 | Peripheral control; protocols; OSI; Integration | Buffers/interrupts/polling/handshaking; TCP/IP protocols; seven layers |
| 10 | (Eval 2) 35–36 | Evaluation/Remediation 2; error detection & parity; internet history | Parity/checksum/CRC/ARQ; internet growth, pros/cons, impact |
| 11 | 37–40 | Connectivity & browsers; social networks & collaboration; e-mail & search; telework, videoconferencing, URLs, cookies | Access technologies; browser features; collaborative tools; e-mail with attachments; search operators; virtual meetings |
| 12 | 41–44 | Integration; e-services; applications/benefits/challenges; CAL & e-learning | E-commerce/e-health/e-government; platform audits; e-learning delivery |
| 13 | 45–46 | Integration; HTML web pages | Digital services programme; simple web pages |

## Cross-module threads

- **From concept to decision:** every module moves from definitions (AI types, network types, protocols, e-services) to justified choices for a Cameroonian organisation – a school, clinic, cooperative or council.
- **Security and trust everywhere:** AI ethics (L2), VPN/firewall/digital signatures (L22), CIA and access control (L23–24), validation (L25), error detection (L35), safe browsing and phishing-proof URLs (L37, L40), cookies and data-protection law (L40), trust in e-services (L42–43).
- **Layers and protocols as a map:** OSI (L33) ties together media (L30), access methods and duplex (L27), transmission types (L29), TCP/IP protocols (L32), error checking (L35), URLs/HTTP (L40, L46).
- **Practical competence:** Teachable Machine (L4), P2P set-up (L18), VPN (L22), permissions and validation (L24–25), Wireshark/commands (L31–33), e-mail and search (L39), virtual meeting (L40), e-service tasks and audits (L42–43), LMS/offline server (L44), HTML pages (L46).
- **Inclusion and the digital divide:** narrowband/USSD channels (L28), rural connectivity choices (L30, L37), accessibility of e-government (L43), offline e-learning (L44), `alt` text (L46).

## Twenty revision questions

1. Define artificial intelligence, distinguish narrow from general AI and give three application areas with Cameroonian examples.
2. State four ethical principles for responsible AI use and explain one risk (bias or privacy) with an example.
3. Describe the stages of developing an AI system and explain why Python is widely used.
4. Compare LAN, MAN, WAN, CAN and PAN on coverage, speed, cost and users.
5. Contrast peer-to-peer and client/server architectures and recommend one for a 12-PC cybercafé and for a 300-user university faculty.
6. Draw star, bus, ring, mesh and tree topologies; give two advantages and two disadvantages of star and mesh.
7. Distinguish hub, switch, router and modem; list the equipment for a two-block school network with justification.
8. Explain intranet, extranet and VPN; describe how a VPN, firewall rules and digital signatures protect a remote worker.
9. Define confidentiality, integrity and availability; give one threat and one control for each.
10. Differentiate validation from verification; give three validation checks and two verification methods for a student-registration form.
11. Explain CSMA/CD and CSMA/CA; classify walkie-talkies, Wi-Fi, switched Ethernet and TV broadcasting by transmission mode.
12. Differentiate broadband from narrowband and choose the appropriate type for farm sensors and for telemedicine.
13. Contrast serial/parallel, synchronous/asynchronous and analog/digital transmission, with a diagram for each pair.
14. Describe UTP, coaxial, fibre, Wi-Fi, microwave and satellite; choose a medium for two buildings 300 m apart and justify.
15. Explain buffers, interrupts, interrupt priorities, polling and handshaking, and identify the technique in three scenarios.
16. Describe the functions of IP, TCP, UDP, HTTP, DNS and DHCP; explain how TCP achieves reliability.
17. List the seven OSI layers with a function, a PDU and a protocol for each; map ten protocols to layers.
18. Compute even and odd parity for two 7-bit codes; explain what parity can and cannot detect and how CRC and ARQ improve on it.
19. Outline the history of the internet (five milestones), compare fibre, 4G and satellite connectivity, and describe six browser privacy features.
20. Describe how an online purchase, a tax e-declaration and a blended e-learning programme operate; label the parts of a URL; write the HTML for a page with a heading, a paragraph, a list, an image, a link and a table.

## Exam preparation guidance

- **Structured answers:** define → explain → example → (where asked) justify a choice with criteria. Use tables for comparisons (network types, topologies, media, protocols, e-learning vs traditional).
- **Diagrams earn marks:** topologies, signal-flow arrows for transmission modes, encapsulation/OSI ladders, waveforms, URL anatomy, e-commerce flowcharts, HTML document trees – practise drawing them quickly and labelling.
- **Practical readiness:** be able to set up sharing between two PCs, configure a VPN client, set folder permissions, apply spreadsheet validation, run `ipconfig`/`ping`/`tracert`/`nslookup`, send an e-mail with attachments, schedule a secure video meeting, audit a website, and hand-code a valid HTML page.
- **Local context:** anchor examples in Cameroon (MoMo/USSD, Camtel fibre, WACS/ACE landings, DHIS2, impots.cm, GCE portals, the 2010 cybercrime and 2024 data-protection laws) – examiners reward relevant, accurate contextualisation.
- **Vocabulary bank:** keep a glossary of the term's key terms (from AI to `alt` attribute) with one-sentence definitions; revise it in pairs with quick-fire questions.
- **Timing:** allocate marks-per-minute; answer all parts; for "evaluate/compare" questions give both sides and a reasoned conclusion.

*End of First Term teaching notes – Upper Sixth ICT. Second Term content is intentionally excluded.*
